%S A399857 6,7,7,9,10,9,11,11,11,11,13,13,14,13,13,15,16,15,15,16,15,17,17,17,
%T A399857 18,17,17,17,19,18,19,19,19,19,18,19,21,21,20,22,21,22,20,21,21
%N A399857 Array read by antidiagonals: T(n,k) is the book Ramsey number R(B_n,B_k).
%C A399857 The book graph B_n is a graph made of n triangles all sharing a single common edge.
%C A399857 Radziszowski (see Links) periodically updates a survey with new results. See there for more information, known terms and bounds, and links to the relevant literature.
%H A399857 Stanisław Radziszowski, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/21">Small Ramsey Numbers</a>, The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (see section 5.3).
%H A399857 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/RamseyNumber.html">Ramsey Number</a>.
%H A399857 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramsey%27s_theorem">Ramsey's theorem</a>.
%H A399857 <a href="/index/Ra#Ramsey">Index entries for sequences related to Ramsey numbers</a>.
%F A399857 T(n,k) = T(k,n).
%F A399857 T(1,k) = 2*k + 3 for k >= 2.
%F A399857 T(n,n) = 4*n + 2 if 4*n + 1 is a prime power.
